{{also|Unto}}
==English==

===Etymology===
By analogy with {{term|until}}, substituting {{term|to}} for {{term|till}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-unto.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Conjunction===
{{en-con}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} (poetic) Up to the [[time]] or [[degree]] that; [[until]]; [[till]].

===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# {{context|archaic|or|poetic|lang=en}} Up to, indicating a motion towards a thing and then stopping at it.
#: ''Sir Gawain rode '''unto''' the nearby castle.''
# {{context|archaic|or|poetic|lang=en}} [[To]], indicating an indirect object
#: ''And the Lord said '''unto''' Moses {{...}}''
#* Sir Thomas Browne
#*: Again, whereas men affirm they perceive an addition of ponderosity in dead bodies, comparing them usually '''unto''' blocks and stones, whensoever they lift or carry them; this accessional preponderancy is rather in appearance than reality.

=====Translations=====
